= [+ date_label +] — A signature with nobody's countersignature on it

**[+ war_top_president +]** makes the appointment himself.
[? if war_top_origin = "tribunal": K 2/26 is cited in the first paragraph of
the presidential statement: the Tribunal said the discretion was his, and he
intends to use it.?][? if war_top_origin != "tribunal": No court granted him anything new. He
simply uses a prerogative that needs no countersignature and dares the
Chancellery to say the act is invalid.?]

The briefing that follows matters more than the appointment. Palace advisers
tell journalists that the government is exhausted, that its polling is a
liability the President did not create, and that the camp will need a different
prime minister before the next election. **[+ war_top_pm +]** answers that a
president who campaigns against his own cabinet is campaigning for the
opposition.

[? if war_top_camp = "lewica": This is our own party tearing at itself. Every
regional secretary, every minister and every club officer is being asked which
of two Left leaders they serve.?][? if war_top_camp = "ko": KO's structures are
being asked to choose between the man who wins presidential elections and the
man who built the party.?][? if war_top_camp = "pis": The right's discipline
has always been the party's, not the Palace's. That is precisely what is now
being tested.?]

[? if left_in_government: Lewica sits in the cabinet the Palace has just
declared expendable.?][? if not left_in_government: Lewica sits outside, with
no ministry to lose and no ability to stop either man.?]

- @war_top_back_cabinet
- @war_top_back_palace
- @war_top_protocol
- @war_top_price
- @war_top_exploit

@war_top_back_cabinet
title: Declare for the Prime Minister and demand the Palace stop briefing
subtitle: A government that cannot rely on its own president cannot govern; say so publicly and take the consequences.
on-arrival: war_top_palace_bloc -= 10; war_top_left_side = "cabinet"; president_relation -= 8; government_coalition_dissent -= 4; government_delivery += 2; public_trust += 1; news_headline = "Lewica declares for the cabinet and tells the Palace to stop briefing"

We back the prime minister in public, on the record and without conditions. The
cabinet stabilises for as long as the Palace believes we mean it. In return we
inherit an enemy who holds the pen for the rest of his term, and who has just
learned exactly how much of the majority he does not control.

- @poland_event_queue: Declare for the cabinet

@war_top_back_palace
title: Declare for the Palace and treat the cabinet as spent
subtitle: Back the more popular office and bet that the camp follows it.
on-arrival: war_top_palace_bloc += 12; war_top_left_side = "palace"; president_relation += 10; government_coalition_dissent += 7; early_election_risk += 4; government_delivery -= 2; news_headline = "Lewica sides with the Palace against a prime minister of its own coalition"

We say what half the camp says privately: this cabinet is finished and the
President is the only asset it has left. The Palace bloc grows and our access to
it becomes real. So does the charge that a governing party has spent a year
undermining the government it sits in.

- @poland_event_queue: Side with the Palace

@war_top_protocol
title: Force a written division of powers between the two offices
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Neither man renounces his claim; both accept a published procedure for appointments, vetoes and joint statements.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The negotiating team cannot be financed.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; war_top_heat -= 12; war_top_palace_bloc -= 4; war_top_left_side = "institutions"; legal_dualism -= 2; constitutional_restraint += 4; government_procedural_restraint += 3; president_judiciary_cooperation += 4; news_headline = "A published protocol divides appointments between Palace and Chancellery"

We put lawyers in a room with both chiefs of staff and refuse to leave with a
communiqué. What emerges is narrow: notification before nominations, published
reasons, no briefing against the other office in the same week as a joint
appearance. It cools the quarrel without resolving who leads the camp, which is
all a protocol can do.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the protocol

@war_top_price
title: Charge both offices for our neutrality
subtitle: Neither man can afford a public Lewica verdict; convert that into legislation and appointments we want.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: war_top_palace_bloc += 4; war_top_heat += 4; war_top_left_side = "price"; resources += 1; government_delivery += 3; party_unity -= 3; public_trust -= 2; news_headline = "Lewica sells its silence in the camp's leadership war"

Both camps send emissaries in the same week; both leave having promised
something. We take a bill, a committee chair and a nomination, and we say
nothing about the leadership of a party that is not ours. Our own left wing
calls it renting out the party's judgement.

- @poland_event_queue: Bank the concessions

@war_top_exploit
title: Make the split the story and take what falls out of it
subtitle: Opposition route. Amplify every leak, and recruit the people the losing side discards.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: war_top_palace_bloc += 6; war_top_heat += 8; war_top_left_side = "exploit"; media_capacity += 4; left_poll_momentum += 0.3; public_trust -= 1; news_headline = "Lewica turns the camp's leadership war into a daily broadcast"

From opposition we cannot arbitrate and should not pretend to. We can make sure
every voter hears that the governing camp spent the year deciding which of two
men it belongs to. Organisers on the losing side start taking our calls.

- @poland_event_queue: Run the split as the story


@war_top_protocol_holds
# prose-audit: Grade B — The quiet landing: a rivalry that never reached a
# rupture is still recorded, with the shared history that kept it survivable.
title: The quarrel that did not become a war
subtitle: Two offices of one camp settle into an arrangement instead of a succession fight
